# End-to-end test suite configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and adjust the values for your Open edX installation:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Configuration is validated at load time: a missing or malformed value fails
# fast with a clear message instead of a misleading test failure. Only
# `.env.example` is committed — never commit a real `.env`.
#
# **Note:** Actual environment variables take precedence over the values set
#           in .env files!

# --- Base URLs (origins) ---------------------------------------------------
# All origins must share one scheme (all http:// or all https://) and one
# registrable parent domain (e.g. *.local.openedx.io), so a single sign-in
# covers every sub-domain. Do not use `localhost` or IP addresses: they have no
# usable sub-domain structure for shared cookies.

# LMS origin. Required.
LMS_BASE_URL=http://local.openedx.io

# Micro-frontend (MFE) host origin — where the authn, learning, account, etc.
# MFEs are served. Required.
APPS_BASE_URL=http://apps.local.openedx.io

# Studio (CMS) origin. Required when the `studio` capability is declared below;
# otherwise optional and unused. Studio authenticates through the LMS session
# (a silent OAuth handshake — no second sign-in), so no separate credentials.
CMS_BASE_URL=http://studio.local.openedx.io

# --- Credentials -----------------------------------------------------------
# Optional admin/staff account used for staff-role coverage and portable test-
# data seeding. Set both together, or omit both. Learner accounts are created by
# the registration flow rather than configured here.
#
# With `studio` declared, the default account backend also uses this account to
# grant course-creator status to the `author` role's fresh account through the
# Django admin (the only path a default install offers). Without it, `author`
# is only available on installs where every user may create courses
# (ENABLE_CREATOR_GROUP off), and Studio coverage skips otherwise.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=edx
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# --- Tenant / content identifiers ------------------------------------------
# Organization short code used when creating or locating content. Optional.
# ORG=OpenedX

# Course the course-completion specs enroll in and work through. Optional: leave
# it unset and those specs skip cleanly. The course itself is a prerequisite of
# the installation, not something the suite creates — import it however your
# install does (on Tutor: `tutor local do importdemocourse`) and put the resulting
# key here. The demo course's key can differ between installs.
# COURSE_KEY=course-v1:OpenedX+DemoX+DemoCourse

# --- Capability declaration ------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated list of capabilities enabled on your installation. Specs
# tagged with a capability run only when it is enabled. Unknown values, or two
# mutually-exclusive capabilities together, fail validation.
# Known capabilities: mfe-authn, frontend-base, instructor-dashboard, discussions,
# notifications, email-inbox, teams, notes, wiki, badges, credly-badges, cohorts,
# content-libraries, content-libraries-v1, courseware-navigation-sidebar,
# courseware-legacy-navigation, catalog-search, studio, taxonomies,
# authoring-sidebar, upload-agreements, ora, drag-and-drop-v2, pdf-xblock, lti,
# edx-sga, scorm, certificates, analytics, rbac, rbac-global,
# rbac-matrix-parity, rbac-error-view-action, certificate-web-view.
#
# `email-inbox` means a mailbox the suite can read is configured (MAIL_PROVIDER,
# below) and the target's outbound mail reaches it, so notification e-mail
# content is asserted. Opt-in; declaring it without MAIL_PROVIDER fails
# validation.
#
# `certificates` means course certificates can be generated on the target. They
# ship with the platform, but the platform-wide switch (Django admin,
# "Certificate generation configurations") is off on a fresh install; the suite
# turns it on once per run through ADMIN_USERNAME/ADMIN_PASSWORD, and skips the
# certificate coverage with a reason when no admin account is configured. Gates
# the instructor dashboard's Certificates tab.
#
# `analytics` is reserved for the Superset/Aspects reports on the instructor
# dashboard (a separate deployment). No spec uses it yet.
#
# `ora`, `drag-and-drop-v2`, `pdf-xblock` and `lti` are component types that ship
# with edx-platform (declare them on a stock install); `edx-sga` and `scorm` are
# separately installed XBlock plugins — declare them only where installed. A
# declared type the target lacks fails its spec rather than skipping.
#
# `studio` means the target has Studio and the course-authoring MFE. It requires
# CMS_BASE_URL, enables the `author` role (a fresh account granted course-creator
# status, see the README) and runs the tests/studio/ tree. Leave it undeclared on
# an LMS-only target and that tree skips cleanly.
#
# `catalog-search` is search and filtering in the catalog MFE, which renders only
# where the LMS's top-level ENABLE_COURSE_DISCOVERY setting is True. Leave it
# undeclared and the catalog specs reach a course by paging the course list
# instead, and the search-specific coverage skips; declare it and that coverage
# runs and fails if the search field is not there. CI declares it for every
# release.
#
# Mutually exclusive pairs (declaring both fails validation): badges /
# credly-badges, and courseware-navigation-sidebar / courseware-legacy-navigation
# (one surface, chosen by the `courseware.enable_navigation_sidebar` waffle flag).
#
# Optional features are off until declared. Stock surfaces a default install
# ships are the other way round — on unless you turn them off with a `-`
# prefix, so forgetting a declaration never silently drops coverage you have:
#   content-libraries  Content libraries v2: the library-authoring MFE, the
#              /api/libraries/v2/ API and reusing library content in a course
#              (verawood and later; Studio's home API reports it as
#              `libraries_v2_enabled`). Declare it to run tests/studio/library/.
#   content-libraries-v1  Legacy (library-v1:) libraries can still be created
#              and migrated to v2 with the migration tool. On by default on
#              the platform; leave it undeclared on an install that has enabled
#              the `disable_legacy_libraries` waffle flag.
#   authoring-sidebar  The Verawood authoring sidebar (Info / Add / Align / Help
#              on the course outline, Info / Add / Align on the unit page).
#              Present on `main` and `verawood`; leave it undeclared on `ulmo`
#              and earlier, where those panels do not exist and the sidebar
#              specs skip. The tag drawer opens from the card kebab regardless,
#              so it stays ungated by this.
#   upload-agreements  The authoring MFE gates the Files / Videos upload
#              controls behind a user-agreement banner. Declare it only on an
#              install that has configured the AGREEMENT_GATING map in its MFE
#              site configuration (CI sets it in the Tutor patch); the specs
#              read the map, seed the matching agreement rows via Django admin
#              (needs an admin account), and skip where either is missing.
#   rbac       Roles and permissions under openedx-authz: the /api/authz/v1/
#              API and the Roles and Permissions console (the admin-console
#              MFE, whose URL the authoring MFE config advertises as
#              ADMIN_CONSOLE_URL). Present on `main` and `verawood`; leave it
#              undeclared on `ulmo` and earlier and tests/rbac/ skips. Library
#              roles need no flag; the course-role coverage turns
#              authz.enable_course_authoring on for its own courses through
#              Django admin, so it also needs an admin account.
#   rbac-global  AuthZ course authoring is enabled for the whole installation
#              (the global waffle flag), not only for the courses a test turns
#              it on for. Declare it only on such a target: it gates the one
#              assertion a per-course override cannot reach, Studio Home's link
#              into the console. CI leaves it undeclared.
#   certificate-web-view  The certificate web view renders on an install with
#              no marketing site. Earlier releases do; on `main` it answers 500
#              (CERT-002), so the case is undeclared there.
#   rbac-error-view-action  The console's not-found view offers a working way
#              back: its action carries a URL and leaves the route. `verawood`
#              does; on `main` the anchor has no href and does nothing
#              (RBAC-008), so the case is undeclared there.
#   rbac-matrix-parity  The console's library permission matrix matches the
#              API's permission list, one row per permission (openedx-authz 1.23
#              and later). `verawood` renders three rows more than the API
#              advertises (wg#609), so the matrix's structure is asserted
#              everywhere and its fidelity to the API only where declared.
#   mfe-authn  The authn MFE owns accounts (native registration, password
#              reset, its own sign-in screens). Turn it off on an install whose
#              identity lives elsewhere — an external auth service reached
#              through a custom ACCOUNT_BACKEND — where those specs have
#              nothing to drive:
# CAPABILITIES=-mfe-authn
#   frontend-base  The MFEs are served in the `frontend-base` shell (one bundled
#              application with a shared header/footer) — `main` and every
#              release cut from it. Turn it off on verawood and earlier, where
#              each MFE runs separately with `frontend-component-header`; the
#              specs about the shell's own chrome then skip. CI does this for
#              those releases via `.ci/openedx-releases.json`:
# CAPABILITIES=-frontend-base
#   instructor-dashboard  The LMS instructor dashboard is the instructor-dashboard
#              MFE (verawood and later; the legacy dashboard redirects to it).
#              Turn it off on ulmo and earlier, which still render the legacy
#              dashboard and lack the /api/instructor/v2/ API; the
#              tests/lms/instructor/ tree then skips. CI does this via
#              `.ci/openedx-releases.json`:
# CAPABILITIES=-instructor-dashboard
#   discussions  Course discussions: the `openedx` discussion provider (the
#              forum) and the discussions MFE. Turn it off on an install
#              without the forum or on the legacy provider. On Tutor the
#              `forum` plugin provides it — enable it and run `tutor local
#              launch` (or `do init --limit=forum`), which creates the forum's
#              search indices:
# CAPABILITIES=-discussions
#   notifications  Course notifications as verawood ships them: on by default,
#              the v3 preferences API and the tray in every MFE header. Turn it
#              off on ulmo and earlier (opt-in flags, older preference APIs, no
#              built-in tray). CI does this via `.ci/openedx-releases.json`:
# CAPABILITIES=-notifications
#
# These defaults should be kept in line with the platform installed defaults
CAPABILITIES=courseware-navigation-sidebar,catalog-search,studio,taxonomies,authoring-sidebar,wiki,cohorts,teams,ora,drag-and-drop-v2,lti,scorm,edx-sga,pdf-xblock,certificates,content-libraries,content-libraries-v1,rbac

# --- Advanced --------------------------------------------------------------
# For providers whose LMS/Studio/MFE origins are NOT same-site (different
# registrable domains). Requires the install to serve SameSite=None +
# Secure cookies with a CORS whitelist. Leave unset for the standard shared
# parent-domain deployment. Accepts: true/false, 1/0, yes/no.
# ALLOW_CROSS_SITE_ORIGINS=false

# Number of parallel Playwright workers. Defaults to 2 locally and 4 in CI.
# Keep it low against a local Tutor: a busy CMS worker (search indexing after
# every publish) evicts author Studio sessions under higher parallelism, so more
# workers trade throughput for flaky StudioSessionExpiredError failures. Accepts
# a positive integer or a percentage of CPU cores like "50%".
# WORKERS=2

# --- Account creation / activation backend --------------------------------
# How a newly-registered account becomes able to sign in. See issue #10.
#   automatic  (default) Registers a throwaway identity and reuses the session
#              registration itself creates, so it works on a default install
#              even when accounts stay inactive until activation. Only the
#              login/logout specs need an account that can sign in separately
#              (SKIP_EMAIL_VALIDATION=True, or use `manual`).
#   manual     Interactive: the suite prompts for an email to register with and
#              for the activation link/token to paste. For targets that enforce
#              email validation and cannot be reconfigured. Run with --workers=1.
#   <plugin>   The `name` of a custom backend loaded from
#              CUSTOM_ACCOUNT_BACKEND_PLUGINS (see src/accounts/README.md).
# ACCOUNT_BACKEND=automatic

# Comma-separated paths of custom account backend plugin modules to load, each
# default-exporting an AccountBackend (or a class/factory for one). Paths are
# resolved from the working directory and must exist. Defaults to none.
# CUSTOM_ACCOUNT_BACKEND_PLUGINS=./plugins/saml-enterprise.plugin.ts

# --- Example plugin: openinbox --------------------------------------------
# `plugins/openinbox.plugin.ts` (shipped as an example) automates what `manual`
# does by hand: it registers with a disposable https://openinbox.io inbox, waits
# for the Open edX activation email, and visits the link. Use it against a target
# that actually sends activation email. Enable with:
#   CUSTOM_ACCOUNT_BACKEND_PLUGINS=./plugins/openinbox.plugin.ts
#   ACCOUNT_BACKEND=openinbox
# The OpenInbox API requires a paid plan. Required when selected. Inboxes are
# deleted as soon as the activation link has been visited, so a run needs only
# as many concurrent inboxes as it has workers.
# OPENINBOX_API_KEY=
# API root, poll budget and poll interval. Optional.
# OPENINBOX_BASE_URL=https://api.openinbox.io/api
# OPENINBOX_POLL_TIMEOUT_MS=90000
# OPENINBOX_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=3000

# --- Mailbox provider (the `email-inbox` capability) -----------------------
# Where the suite reads e-mail the target sends, for notification-mail coverage.
# A provider is a plugin (see src/mail/README.md); two ship in plugins/:
#   mailpit    A Mailpit catcher the target's SMTP points at (what the Tutor CI
#              workflow runs). Needs MAILPIT_BASE_URL.
#   openinbox  A disposable openinbox.io inbox, for a target that sends real
#              mail to the internet. Needs OPENINBOX_API_KEY (above).
# CUSTOM_MAIL_PROVIDER_PLUGINS=./plugins/mailpit.plugin.ts
# MAIL_PROVIDER=mailpit
# Mailpit's web/API origin, as the machine running the suite reaches it.
# MAILPIT_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8025
# Domain of the addresses the plugin mints, and its poll interval. Optional.
# MAILPIT_DOMAIN=e2e.test
# MAILPIT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1000

# --- Registration rate limit (server-side) --------------------------------
# Open edX caps registrations per IP via REGISTRATION_RATELIMIT (default
# "60/7d"). Each run creates several accounts, so frequent runs exhaust it and
# registration fails with HTTP 403 forbidden-request. For repeatedly-tested
# targets, raise it to a short self-resetting window (e.g. REGISTRATION_RATELIMIT
# = "100/m") and restart the LMS. This is a target Django setting, not a suite
# env var. See the README "Registration rate limit" section.
